"A physical network diagram documents devices, physical ports, and the actual links connecting them.\n\nUse when: Review cabling, port connectivity, or device replacement. Explain physical redundancy and shared failure points.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need only IP routing or policy allowance.\n\nVisual grammar: Device shapes identify switches, routers, endpoints, or patch panels. Lines join named physical ports and optionally state medium or verified link rate. Padded groups encode racks, rooms, or sites when relevant.\n\nBuild: Choose a site scope and a verified inventory date. Place devices according to connection clarity rather than decorative geography. Connect exact ports and label medium or link attributes from evidence. Route cables in distinct channels and use continuation references for dense inter-rack links.\n\nCheck: Both ends of each link identify the intended ports. Aggregated links and redundant paths are not silently collapsed. Configured speed and measured throughput remain distinct. Cable routes, port names, and rack titles do not intersect.\n\nPitfalls: Drawing logical overlay connections as if they were physical cables. Inferring independent failure paths from two lines that share the same conduit.\n\nExample: Two access switches connect through separate labeled ports to a core pair, with patch-panel links shown explicitly.\n\nAlternatives: Logical network diagram, Deployment diagram."
